@Files
.
=FILES
.
.OPEN opens a file on drive 8 and returns its handle. PRINT # writes to the
.handle, INPUT # and LINPUT # read from it, and CLOSE closes it. READ and
.WRITE move bytes between the file and memory.
.
:DIM F AS UWORD
:F = OPEN("LOG.TXT", O.WRITE)
:IF F = NOFILE
:  PRINT "CANNOT OPEN"
:  END
:ENDIF
:PRINT #F, "SCORE"; 120
:CLOSE F
.
.Close every file before the program ends.
.
=OPEN
.
.F = OPEN(name$, mode) returns a handle, a UWORD. The name is a literal or
.any string expression. The mode is one of three names, written as the
.argument itself:
.
.  - O.READ reads the file.
.  - O.WRITE makes the file, and replaces one that is there.
.  - O.APPEND writes at the end of the file, and makes the file when it is
.    missing.
.
.Any other mode stops the compile with MODE MUST BE O.READ, O.WRITE OR
.O.APPEND.
.
.OPEN returns NOFILE when the file cannot be opened: the file is missing for
.O.READ, the drive reports an error, or no handle is free. NOFILE is a
.built-in constant, 65535. Up to 12 files can be open at once, as handles 3
.to 14.
.
=PRINT # and CLOSE
.
.PRINT #F, items works as PRINT does, and writes to the file.
.
.  - Each line ends with a CR, 13. A trailing ; leaves out the CR.
.  - A comma between items writes a "," character, not spaces to a print
.    zone.
.  - A number has a space before and after it, as on the screen. PRINT #F,
.    "AB"; 5, "CD" writes AB 5 ,CD and a CR.
.  - Handles 0, 1 and 2 are the screen: PRINT #1, "HI" is PRINT "HI".
.  - A handle that is not open stops the program with ?FILE NOT OPEN ERROR AT
.    $xxxx.
.
.CLOSE F closes the file. CLOSE of a handle that is not open, NOFILE
.included, does nothing.
.
=INPUT #, LINPUT # and EOF
.
.INPUT #F, variables reads fields from the file. LINPUT #F, L$ reads a whole
.line, commas and quotes included. EOF(F) is 1 when the next read would
.return nothing, and 0 otherwise.
.
:DIM F AS UWORD, L$ * 80
:F = OPEN("DATA.TXT", O.READ)
:IF F = NOFILE
:  PRINT "NO FILE"
:  END
:ENDIF
:DO WHILE NOT EOF(F)
:  LINPUT #F, L$
:  PRINT L$
:LOOP
:CLOSE F
.
.  - A field ends at a comma or at the end of a line. A number variable gets
.    the field's value as VAL reads it. A LONG or ULONG variable is read in
.    32 bits.
.  - The rest of the line stays for the next read. Keyboard INPUT drops it.
.  - No prompt string is allowed after #F,.
.  - A line ends at CR, at LF, or at CR LF, so files from the X16, Unix and
.    DOS read the same. The line ending is not stored.
.  - A field or line longer than the string is cut, and the rest of it is
.    skipped.
.  - At the end of the file a string gets "" and a number gets 0.
.  - EOF is 1 right after an empty file is opened. EOF(NOFILE) is 1.
.  - Handles 0, 1 and 2 are the screen and cannot be read.
.  - A read from a handle that is not open, 0 to 2 included, stops the
.    program with ?FILE NOT OPEN ERROR AT $xxxx.
.
.See INPUT and LINPUT.
.
=READ and WRITE
.
.N = READ(F, address, max) reads up to max bytes from the file into memory at
.the address. It returns the count read, a UWORD. WRITE F, address, count
.writes count bytes from memory at the address to the file.
.
:DIM F AS UWORD, N AS UWORD, B(100) AS UBYTE
:F = OPEN("DATA.BIN", O.READ)
:N = READ(F, @B, 100)
:CLOSE F
:F = OPEN("COPY.BIN", O.WRITE)
:WRITE F, @B, N
:CLOSE F
.
.  - The address is any UWORD expression. @B is the address of an array or a
.    variable. @S$ + 2 is the text of a string, after its max and length
.    bytes. A number such as $A000 is a fixed address.
.  - Bytes move as they are, CR, LF and 0 included. No line ending is added
.    or dropped.
.  - READ returns 0 at the end of the file. A max of 0 reads nothing and
.    returns 0. A count of 0 writes nothing.
.  - READ, INPUT #, LINPUT # and EOF can be mixed on the same handle. EOF
.    stays right after a READ.
.  - WRITE to handles 0, 1 and 2 goes to the screen. WRITE 1, @S$ + 2, 2
.    prints the first two characters of S$.
.  - READ from handles 0 to 2, or from a handle that is not open, NOFILE
.    included, stops the program with ?FILE NOT OPEN ERROR AT $xxxx. WRITE to
.    a handle 3 to 14 that is not open, or to NOFILE, does the same.
.  - Each byte costs a KERNAL CHRIN and READST to read, or a CHROUT to write.
.
.See Arrays and Strings for @name.
.
=SEE ALSO
.
